WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.880
A Canadian University lab is teaming up with the Canadian Space Agency to explore how living systems could manufacture materials beyond Earth. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 177. The University of Lethbridge Lab will investigate how microorganisms and engineered cells can produce useful compounds and materials in microgravity or on other planetary surfaces. The project focuses on extraterrestrial bio-manufacturing techniques

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:55.620
that could support long duration missions by generating food, pharmaceuticals, or construction materials on site. Researchers aim to understand how altered gravity and radiation environments affect cellular processes used in production. The collaboration builds on existing Canadian expertise in space biology and seeks to develop closed loop systems that reduce reliance on resupply from Earth.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:21.660
Next steps include ground-based simulations followed by potential experiments on the International Space Station. The work draws on the lab's prior studies of cellular responses to space conditions. Canadian Space Agency support will help translate ground findings into flight ready hardware. While Canadian researchers work on growing what crews need, another team is tackling what to do with everything that gets discarded.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:48.220
The composites for extraterrestrial recycling by engineering the reuse and upcycling of Zotech team from the Massachusetts Institute of Technology received $775,000 for its system that converts mixed waste into reusable powder. The approach grinds fabrics, plastics, foam, and metals into a fine material that can be reformed into new parts or structures during lunar or deep space missions.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:02:10.360
The National Aeronautics and Space Administration designed the challenge to address the growing problem of waste accumulation on long duration flights where every kilogram of trash must be managed without return to Earth. The winning technology emphasizes upcycling rather than simple disposal, turning discarded items into feedstock for additive manufacturing.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:30.660
The team includes undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. Follow on work will test the process in relevant environments to refine particle size and material properties. The award reflects the agency's priority on sustainable logistics for crews operating far from Earth.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:47.880
The system turns everyday trash into manufacturing feedstock by grinding mixed materials to a uniform particle size. This approach could let crews on the moon or Mars turn packaging and worn equipment into new tools or habitat components without waiting for resupply.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:56.740
The award funds further testing to ensure the powder works reliably in additive manufacturing systems under lunar gravity and vacuum conditions.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:06.440
The process handles fabrics, plastics, foam, and metals together, avoiding the need for separate sorting streams that would add complexity on long missions.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:17.380
What remains unclear is how radiation exposure over months or years will alter the recycled material strength and chemical stability once it leaves Earth's protective environment.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:29.820
Imagine every kilogram of waste generated on a lunar base must either be stored forever or turned into something useful because lifting replacement materials from Earth costs thousands of dollars per kilogram.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:41.460
The challenge winner shows one path forward by grinding mixed plastics, fabrics, and metals into a fine powder that can be fed into three-dimensional printers to create new parts on demand.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:54.780
In practice, a single crew member might produce several kilograms of trash each week, and over a year that adds up to hundreds of kilograms that could instead become brackets, insulation panels, or even simple tools.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:03.620
The $775,000 prize reflects how seriously the agency takes the logistics problem of sustaining crews far from resupply.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:25.520
The approach avoids pre-sorting by treating all common spacecraft materials as a single feedstock stream. Yet the open question is whether repeated grinding and reforming will preserve the mechanical properties needed for structural parts or whether radiation and thermal cycling will make the recycled material brittle in ways we cannot yet predict from ground tests alone.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:41.700
From turning waste into resources, we move to recognizing the people who fly the missions that create that waste. Canadian astronaut Jeremy Hansen was awarded the Congressional Space Medal of Honor for his role on the Artemis II crew that flew around the moon.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:47.640
The honor marks the first time the medal has gone to someone outside the agency's astronaut corps.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:55.900
Hansen's selection highlights the international nature of the Artemis program and Canada's contributions through the Canadian Space Agency.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:02.480
The medal recognizes the crew's successful demonstration of Orion spacecraft systems in lunar orbit.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:12.840
Future Artemis missions will build on this flight to advance crude lunar landing capabilities. Hansen becomes the first non-agency astronaut to receive the distinction.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:25.920
The award ceremony took place at the agency's Johnson Space Center in Houston. With Canadian contributions to exploration in the spotlight, commercial operators are inviting the public to help shape their next vehicles.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:34.960
Virgin Galactic extended the deadline for naming its next generation space plane, giving the public until Thursday to submit and vote on suggestions.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:44.820
The company retired its earlier spaceship to vehicle after completing its first commercial flight in 2023 and has not conducted another since 2024.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:54.680
The new vehicles are intended for suborbital research and tourism flights. The naming contest aims to engage the public in the company's next phase of operations.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:06:04.460
Results will be announced after the voting period closes. The extension allows more time for enthusiasts to participate in shaping the identity of the upcoming fleet.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:18.360
While naming contests play out on the ground, orbital hardware is already coming back from flight. Space X completed recovery of the most recent starship test article from the Indian Ocean, roughly a month after its launch.

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:30.520
The spacecraft is now being transported back to star base for inspection and analysis. Engineers will examine the vehicle's heat shield and structure to assess performance during reentry and splashdown.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:41.080
This marks the largest salvage operation the company has conducted for a starship prototype. Data from the recovered hardware will inform upgrades ahead of future test flights.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:53.160
The operation demonstrates the feasibility of retrieving large orbital vehicles after water landings. Post-flight analysis will focus on thermal protection system integrity and structural loads.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:58.820
From hardware returning to earth, we turn to satellites that stay in orbit to watch our planet.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:07.720
The agency posted launch footage of Sentinel-6B, which lifted off aboard a Space X Falcon 9 from Vandenberg Space Force Base.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:21.400
The satellite continues the long-term record of sea level measurements begun by earlier missions in the series. Sentinel-6B will monitor ocean height, temperature, and salinity to support climate and weather research.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:33.000
The videos allow the public to review the full-ascent sequence and spacecraft separation. The mission extends the Jason series of ocean observing satellites that have operated since 1992.

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:40.040
Data from Sentinel-6B will feed into models used by weather forecasters and climate scientists worldwide.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:46.380
While ocean data flows from orbit, leadership on the ground checks in with the crew maintaining the station.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:56.040
President Trump spoke with the Expedition 75 astronauts after presenting the Congressional Space Medal of Honor to the Artemis II crew at the same location.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:01.400
The conversation covered ongoing station operations and future exploration goals.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:07.500
Expedition 75 continues science and maintenance activities aboard the International Space Station.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:13.240
The call occurred during the president's visit to the agency's Johnson Space Center in Houston, Texas.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:19.180
Crew members discussed daily research tasks and preparations for upcoming resupply missions.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:24.320
The exchange highlighted coordination between national leadership and orbital operations.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:30.860
Space news continues to show steady progress on the practical challenges of living and working beyond Earth.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:37.700
Keep an eye on the next round of Starship Testing, where the data from this recovery will shape the next vehicle's design.

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:40.540
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

